00:00:00,103 --> 00:00:04,421
What a Survey Can Tell Us A popular claim on campus was

2
00:00:04,434 --> 00:00:09,829
that online courses would inevitably improve learning To examine its validity a

3
00:00:09,842 --> 00:00:14,974
research team designed a questionnaire and selected a random sample The students

4
00:00:14,987 --> 00:00:21,395
recorded each response without changing an inconvenient datum They distinguished quantitative measures

5
00:00:21,592 --> 00:00:28,000
such as study hours from qualitative observations about motivation The initial statistics

6
00:00:28,013 --> 00:00:34,513
showed a positive correlation between regular attendance and examination performance However a

7
00:00:34,526 --> 00:00:39,882
correlation did not establish a cause A learner's prior knowledge might influence

8
00:00:39,895 --> 00:00:45,132
both variables Nor was the sample homogeneous a junior student could have

9
00:00:45,145 --> 00:00:51,013
different priorities from someone approaching graduation The team therefore refused to generalize

10
00:00:51,026 --> 00:00:57,671
too quickly A numerical coefficient offered useful information but its interpretation required

11
00:00:57,684 --> 00:01:03,855
caution An analytic approach or an analytical one involves examining how separate

12
00:01:03,868 --> 00:01:09,789
factors interact The final report described the study's scope identified a possible

13
00:01:09,803 --> 00:01:16,250
flaw and acknowledged that some conclusions remained uncertain Only by reporting limitations

14
00:01:16,263 --> 00:01:21,579
openly could the researchers make their recommendation credible The exercise taught them

15
00:01:21,592 --> 00:01:26,263
that an honest qualification strengthens an argument instead of making it worthless
